BT /F2 18 Tf 48 770 Td (Reliability: the most important questions) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 737 Td (11. What problem does the transactional outbox solve?) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 715 Td (Saving an order in SQL and independently publishing to Kafka creates a dual-write gap:) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 699 Td (one operation may succeed while the other fails. The outbox stores the order and event) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 683 Td (in the same SQL transaction. A separate publisher sends pending events. This) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 667 Td (guarantees a committed order has a durable publication intent, but a crash can still) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 651 Td (cause an event to be sent twice.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 621 Td (12. What if the publisher crashes after Kafka accepts the event?) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 599 Td (If SQL has not yet recorded that the event was published, the publisher will send it) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 583 Td (again after restart. This is why consumers must tolerate redelivery. Producer) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 567 Td (idempotence helps Kafka producer retries, but it does not make Kafka and SQL one) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 551 Td (atomic transaction.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 521 Td (13. How do you prevent duplicate HTTP orders?) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 499 Td (The client sends an Idempotency-Key. A database uniqueness constraint permits only one) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 483 Td (order per key. Retrying the same key with the same SKU and quantity returns the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 467 Td (existing order. Reusing the key with a different payload returns HTTP 409. A new key) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 451 Td (represents a new request even if its payload is identical.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 421 Td (14. How do consumers prevent duplicate effects?) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 399 Td (A unique inbox marker identifies a consumer and event ID. That marker, stock changes) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 383 Td (or projection changes, and any outgoing event commit in the same transaction. A) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 367 Td (redelivered event with a committed marker is ignored. Additional order-ID constraints) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 351 Td (protect business effects such as reservations and notification records.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 321 Td (15. Why must the inbox and business update share a transaction?) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 299 Td (If the inbox commits first and the business update fails, a retry could incorrectly) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 283 Td (skip unfinished work. If the business update commits first and the inbox fails, a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 267 Td (retry could apply the effect twice. Committing both together means success records) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 251 Td (both, and failure rolls back both.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 221 Td (16. Do you guarantee exactly-once processing?) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 199 Td (I describe this as at-least-once delivery with idempotent SQL effects. Events can be) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 183 Td (delivered more than once, but successful duplicate deliveries do not repeat the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 167 Td (protected business updates. I do not claim globally exactly-once delivery across Kafka) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 48 151 Td (and PostgreSQL.) Tj ET
